        WHAT IT IS FOR: hiding the Submit_Sale_Decision quick action on Disposition_Record_Page
        while this approval is genuinely pending. The four actions write and clear
        Disposition__c.Approval_Pending__c, which four Dynamic Actions on that page read. The
        server side guard in DispositionApprovalService is UNCHANGED and remains the
        authoritative check: this is a UX affordance layered over a working refusal.

        🔴 initialSubmissionActions AND recallActions APPEAR NOWHERE ELSE IN THIS REPO. Before
        this change, a grep across all seven files in force-app/main/default/approvalProcesses/
        returned ZERO occurrences of either. Only finalApprovalActions and finalRejectionActions
        were proven here. What the shape below rests on, stated as evidence rather than assumed:
          1. The Metadata API ApprovalProcess reference types BOTH new elements as
             ApprovalAction, the SAME type as finalApprovalActions and finalRejectionActions,
             and defines ApprovalAction as one repeating field `action` of type
             WorkflowActionReference[]. So the child shape below is not a guess; it is the same
             child shape already deployed in this repo twice.
          2. Element ORDER in these files is alphabetical. That was an observation about five
             files; the Metadata API reference presents the type's fields in the same
             alphabetical order, which corroborates it independently. initialSubmissionActions
             therefore sits after finalRejectionRecordLock and before label; recallActions after
             processOrder and before recordEditability.

        🔴 A GREEN DEPLOY IS NOT PROOF THAT recallActions FIRES, AND THE FAILURE MODE IS THE
        DANGEROUS DIRECTION. A recallActions block that deploys and never executes leaves
        Approval_Pending__c stuck TRUE on a record that is no longer locked and no longer has an
        approval, hiding its Submit button PERMANENTLY with no route to resubmit and nothing
        logged anywhere. That is strictly worse than the noisy refusal this change replaces.
        REQUIRED BEHAVIOURAL READBACK, on this process alone, before the other three are
        deployed:
            1. submit a test disposition at Disposition Readiness;
            2. confirm Approval_Pending__c reads TRUE and the Submit button is gone;
            3. RECALL it from the Approval History component in the page header;
            4. confirm Approval_Pending__c reads FALSE and the button is back.
        If step 4 fails, revert this file rather than substituting another mechanism; the
        alternatives were analysed and rejected, and the safe fallback is today's behaviour.

        WHY RECALL IS NOT A HYPOTHETICAL HERE. allowRecall stays true, recordEditability stays
        AdminOnly, and the Approval History component was added to Disposition_Record_Page on
        2026-08-19 (code review C-3) SPECIFICALLY so the Recall button stopped being unreachable
        from the UI. Recall is now a routine one click operation on this page, so a design that
        left the button hidden after one would break a workflow that change went out of its way
        to enable. recallActions is the ONLY hook for the event: a recall fires no rejection
        action, leaves no ProcessInstance status the record can read, and has no trigger context.

        ⚠ THE REQUIREMENT IS "HIDDEN WHILE PENDING", NOT "HIDDEN UNTIL REJECTED". The flag's
        lifetime is exactly the ProcessInstance.Status = 'Pending' window, and all four terminal
        transitions clear it. That is the deliberate difference from a flag whose clearing
        depends on one specific exit path.

        ⚠ TWO SIMILARLY NAMED FIELDS ARE NOW WRITTEN BY THIS FILE. Approval_Advance_Pending__c
        (the D-1 semaphore, true on FINAL APPROVAL) and Approval_Pending__c (true while PENDING).
        They are opposites in time. Read the banner on
        objects/Disposition__c/fields/Approval_Pending__c.field-meta.xml before editing either.

        DEPLOY ORDER for this change: objects/Disposition__c/fields/Approval_Pending__c
        .field-meta.xml, then workflows/Disposition__c.workflow-meta.xml, then this file, then
        the two Disposition permission sets, then the FlexiPage LAST. Reversing the last two
        hides all four Submit buttons for everyone, silently.

